@startuml ngram-training
' Two-pass n-gram training: Pass 1 counts every order in parallel (lock-free atomic
' increments); Pass 2 derives the Kneser-Ney continuation statistics; the discounts are
' then estimated from the count-of-counts and sealed into an immutable NgramModel.
' Render: plantuml -tsvg docs/diagrams/ngram-training.puml
' Source of truth for Figure 1 in docs/components/ngram/overview.md
skinparam backgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam linetype ortho
skinparam rectangle {
  FontColor white
  FontStyle bold
  BorderColor #263238
  roundCorner 12
}
skinparam ArrowColor #37474F
skinparam ArrowFontColor #37474F
title N-gram training — two passes over the corpus

legend top right
  |= Color |= Concept |
  |<#1565C0>     | Statistical LM core |
  |<#00838F>     | Storage / persistence |
  |<#546E7A>     | Corpus / external |
endlegend

rectangle "**CorpusReader**\nPlaintextReader · Wikipedia · Google Books" as corpus #546E7A
rectangle "**Tokenizer**\nsentence → [w₁ … w_m]" as tok #546E7A

rectangle "**Pass 1 — count**\nfor every order k ∈ 1..n\nemit each k-gram window\ncount.fetch_add(1, Relaxed)" as p1 #1565C0
rectangle "**Pass 2 — continuation stats**\nN₁₊(•, w) = |{h : c(h·w) > 0}|\nN₁₊(h, •) = |{w : c(h·w) > 0}|" as p2 #1565C0
rectangle "**Discount estimation**\nn₁ n₂ n₃ n₄ → Y, D₁, D₂, D₃₊\nN₁₊(•,•) = Σ_w N₁₊(•, w)" as disc #1565C0
rectangle "**NgramModel**\ntrie · smoothing · |V| · total_count\n(immutable — Send + Sync)" as model #1565C0

rectangle "**Vocabulary ARTrie**\nword → u64 index (lock-free CAS)\nWAL + crash recovery" as vocab #00838F
rectangle "**N-gram trie**\nLEB128 varint keys → NgramEntry\nDynamicDawgChar · PathMap · ARTrie" as trie #00838F

corpus --> tok
tok --> p1
p1 --> vocab : intern words
p1 --> trie : insert / increment
p1 --> p2 : counts complete
p2 --> trie : set N₁₊ fields
p2 --> disc
trie --> disc : count-of-counts
disc --> model
trie --> model : moved in
vocab -[#00838F,dashed]-> trie : encodes keys

note bottom of p1 #E3F2FD
  Rayon splits the corpus into batches; NgramEntry's
  three fields are atomics, so workers never lock.
end note

note bottom of p2 #E3F2FD
  Continuation counts are what make Kneser-Ney
  measure a word's *versatility* rather than its
  raw frequency (the "San Francisco" effect).
end note

@enduml
